---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { wi, site } from '../data/site.js';
import { criteria } from '../data/providers.js';
const faqs = [
  ['Which prewarmed inbox provider is best?', 'It depends on which of these you need most: aging, warming, an included domain, replacement terms and platform coverage. This page ranks the criteria rather than the companies, because we sell one of the companies.'],
  ['When should I not buy prewarmed inboxes?', 'When your launch is more than about two months out and you already run warming yourself. Prewarmed buys time and removes setup work. If you have the time and the setup is not a problem for you, you are paying for something you do not need.'],
  ['Is prewarmed just a rebranded aged domain?', 'From some sellers, yes. Aged means old. Prewarmed means aged and then used to send and receive real mail under published authentication. Ask how many days of each, and treat a vague answer as a no.'],
  ['Are the warmup networks real traffic?', 'Warming pools are accounts mailing each other. That is not the same as a customer conversation, and nobody should claim otherwise. What it does do is establish a normal sending pattern before you start, which is what the receiving side is checking for.'],
  ['Does buying prewarmed guarantee inbox placement?', 'No, and a provider who says it does is selling you something they cannot deliver. Infrastructure decides whether you get a fair hearing. Your list and your copy decide the rest.'],
];
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })) },
  ],
};
---
<Base title="Best Prewarmed Inboxes — How to Choose, and When Not to Buy"
      description="The criteria that separate a real prewarmed inbox from a repackaged aged domain, the questions to ask any seller, and when not to buy."
      schema={schema}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> <span class="muted">›</span> <span class="muted">Choosing</span></p>
      <p class="eyebrow">Buyer's guide</p>
      <h1>Best prewarmed inboxes: how to choose</h1>
      <p class="lede">This page ranks criteria, not companies. We are one of the companies, and a ranking from a seller is worth what you would expect.</p>
      <p class="disclosure">{site.disclosure} The method is on <a href="/methodology/">the methodology page</a>, and the directory of who sells what is at <a href="/providers/">providers</a>.</p>

      <h2>The questions that settle it</h2>
      <p>Most of the difference between providers collapses into a few answers. Ask them before price comes up, and ask them of every seller in the conversation.</p>
      <ol class="asks">
        <li><strong>How many days was the domain registered before its first send?</strong><span>Under 30 and you are buying a domain that is exposed to an early blocklisting, which is the hardest kind to reverse. This is the step most often skipped, because waiting costs the seller stock turnover.</span></li>
        <li><strong>How many days did it send and receive real mail before I get it?</strong><span>Age alone builds no reputation. A domain registered in 2011 that has never sent anything is an unknown sender. If the answer is a number of days, good. If it is "it's aged", they have answered a different question.</span></li>
        <li><strong>What are the replacement terms, in writing, before I order?</strong><span>Every provider has domains that stop landing. What differs is what happens next and how fast. A seller who will not put it in writing beforehand will not be better about it afterwards.</span></li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The criteria, in the order they matter</h2>
      <dl class="crit">
        {criteria.map(([h, b], i) => (
          <div><dt><span class="n mono">{i + 1}</span>{h}</dt><dd>{b}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2>When you should not buy prewarmed inboxes</h2>
      <p>Prewarmed infrastructure solves two problems: time to launch, and the setup work. If neither of those is your problem, you are paying for something you do not need, and you should not.</p>
      <p><strong>You have three or four weeks and do not value the speed.</strong> If nothing has to go out until well into next quarter, fresh infrastructure is a perfectly reasonable purchase. Buy fresh mailboxes, run the aging and the warming yourself, and keep the difference. The <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> will tell you whether your date actually leaves room.</p>
      <p><strong>You already run warming well.</strong> Some teams have the tooling, the routine and the patience. For them the 51 days is a background process rather than a project, and paying someone else to have already done it is paying for convenience they do not need.</p>
      <p><strong>Your problem is the list, not the infrastructure.</strong> This is the big one. If your replies stopped because you are emailing bad data or your copy reads like a template, new inboxes will burn exactly as fast as the old ones did and you will have spent money to arrive in the same place. Rule out the list first. A provider who lets you buy without asking about your list is not being helpful.</p>
      <p><strong>You have not found out why the last domain went.</strong> Replacing capacity before you understand the failure repeats the failure on cleaner infrastructure.</p>

      <h2>The objections, answered straight</h2>
      <h3>"It is just an aged domain with a new label"</h3>
      <p>From some sellers, that is exactly what it is, and the way to find out is question two above. Aged and prewarmed describe different things, and only one of them involves the domain having sent anything. A domain bought second hand, registered years ago, abandoned repeatedly and last used for spam is worse than one registered last month — and the listing will only mention the year.</p>
      <h3>"Warmup pools are fake traffic"</h3>
      <p>Warming pools are accounts mailing each other, and pretending that is indistinguishable from customer correspondence would be dishonest. What it establishes is a normal pattern — mail going out, mail coming back, replies happening — before you start sending to strangers. That pattern is what the receiving side looks at. It is a real signal produced by an artificial process, and both halves of that sentence are true.</p>
      <h3>"Would I not be better warming fresh mailboxes myself?"</h3>
      <p>Often, yes — see the section above. The honest framing is that prewarmed primarily buys time to launch and removes setup work. It is not magic and it is not a different class of mailbox. It is the same mailbox with 51 days already spent on it.</p>
      <h3>"What about the ownership history of the domain?"</h3>
      <p>A fair question, and the reason to ask what a provider checks before acquisition. Drop history and blocklist exposure both matter and neither is visible from the age. Our own answer is on the <a href="/prewarmed-domains/">domains page</a>; ask anyone else the same thing.</p>

      <h2>What no provider can sell you</h2>
      <p>Infrastructure decides whether your mail gets a fair hearing. It does not decide whether anyone replies. A clean, aged, warmed domain pointed at a list you have not verified, carrying copy nobody wants to read, will fail — slightly more slowly than a bad one, which is the worst possible outcome because it takes longer to learn from.</p>
      <p>Anyone promising inbox placement as a guarantee is selling you something they do not control.</p>

      <section class="ours">
        <h2>What we ship, for comparison</h2>
        <p>So you can run the questions on us: domains aged 30 days before first send, then 21 days of real sending and receiving; the domain included at no cost; Google Workspace, Microsoft 365 and Azure tenants; delivered same day. On replacement: there is no automatic swap, and you should get that answer in writing from every seller you are weighing, including us.</p>
        <p class="cta"><a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={wi('/', { page: 'best-prewarmed-inboxes', placement: 'body' })}>Get inboxes at Warm Inboxes</a> <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="/calculator/">Work out what you need →</a></p>
      </section>

      <Faq faqs={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 780px; }
  .crumb { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--text-2); margin: 0 0 26px; }
  h1 { margin: 12px 0 18px; }
  h2 { margin: 52px 0 16px; }
  h3 { margin: 30px 0 10px; font-size: 18px; }
  p { color: var(--text-2); }
  p strong { color: var(--text); }
  .disclosure { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 18px; }
  .asks { list-style: none; counter-reset: a; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .asks li { counter-increment: a; position: relative; padding: 0 0 24px 44px; }
  .asks li::before { content: counter(a); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: 29px; height: 29px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600; display: grid; place-items: center; }
  .asks strong { display: block; color: var(--text); font-size: 16.5px; margin-bottom: 7px; }
  .asks span { display: block; font-size: 15px; color: var(--text-2); }
  .crit > div { padding: 15px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .crit dt { font-weight: 600; color: var(--text); font-size: 16.5px; margin-bottom: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }
  .crit .n { color: var(--accent); font-size: 13px; }
  .crit dd { margin: 0 0 0 26px; color: var(--text-2); font-size: 15.5px; }
  .ours { margin-top: 56px; background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: 26px 28px; }
  .ours h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  .cta { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 22px; }
</style>
